\section*{Recursive File Search Utility in C}

To illustrate C in practice, this section provides a detailed account of implementing a simple recursive file search utility in C. The program’s goal is analogous to the Unix \texttt{find} command or a simplified file tree search: given a starting directory and a target filename (or pattern), it traverses the directory hierarchy recursively to locate files that match the target. This example will highlight how C handles tasks like directory I/O, string manipulation, recursion, and memory management in a real program.

\subsection*{Program Overview}

The utility will take two inputs – a directory path to start from, and a name (or substring) to search for in filenames. It will explore the directory and its subdirectories, printing out paths of files that match the query. This involves a classic recursive algorithm: for each item in a directory, if it is a subdirectory, recursively search inside it; if it is a file, check if it matches the search term.

\subsection*{Using System Calls and Libraries}

In C, directory traversal relies on system-specific APIs. On POSIX systems (Unix, Linux, macOS), the \texttt{<dirent.h>} library provides functions to read directories. The code begins by calling \texttt{opendir()} on the starting directory path, which returns a \texttt{DIR*} handle (akin to how \texttt{fopen} returns a \texttt{FILE*} for files)[55]. We must check this handle – if \texttt{opendir} returns \texttt{NULL}, the directory could not be opened (perhaps due to permissions or it doesn’t exist), and the program should handle that (e.g., by printing an error and skipping that branch).

Assuming we have a valid directory stream, we then enter a loop using \texttt{readdir()}. This function returns a pointer to a \texttt{struct dirent} representing the next directory entry (file or subdirectory) in the opened directory[55]. Each \texttt{dirent} contains fields including the name of the entry (\texttt{d\_name}) and a type field (\texttt{d\_type}) that indicates if the entry is a regular file, directory, etc., on systems that support it[56]. We iterate over all entries until \texttt{readdir} returns \texttt{NULL}, which signifies no more entries[55].

For each entry, the program needs to distinguish between directories and files. Typically, the \texttt{d\_type} field can be used (e.g., \texttt{DT\_DIR} for directories)[57][56], but not all systems reliably fill this field (in some cases one might need to use the \texttt{stat()} system call to get the file type). In our scenario, we assume it’s available.

We also must skip the pseudo-entries \texttt{"."} and \texttt{".."} which represent the current and parent directory – recursing on those would cause infinite loops[58][59]. After skipping those, when a subdirectory is found, the program constructs a new path for that subdirectory (e.g., if current directory is \texttt{/home/user} and entry is \texttt{docs}, new path becomes \texttt{/home/user/docs}) and then calls the recursive function on that new path[57]. This is the essence of recursion in this utility: the function \texttt{searchDirectory(char* path, char* query)} will call itself on subdirectories.

When a file (regular file) is encountered, the program checks if its name matches the search term. If we are searching for an exact filename, a simple string comparison (\texttt{strcmp}) between \texttt{d\_name} and the target name could be done. If we allow substring matches, one could use \texttt{strstr} (from \texttt{<string.h>}) to see if the target appears in the filename. For simplicity, let’s consider exact matches. If there’s a match, the program will output the full path of the file found (for example, print to standard output). The printing might use:

\begin{verbatim}
printf("%s/%s\n", currentPath, d_name)
\end{verbatim}

to show the path. In the recursion, since we accumulate the path as we go deeper (as seen in the code snippet below), each printed result will be a fully qualified path.

\subsection*{Memory and String Handling}

Constructing the new path for recursive calls requires careful string handling. A buffer (character array) is used to build strings like \texttt{"parentDir/childName"}. In C, one must ensure the buffer is large enough to hold the resulting path and the null terminator. A common pattern is to use a fixed-size array (say \texttt{char path[1024]}) and \texttt{snprintf} or \texttt{sprintf} to concatenate directory and file names[57]. If paths can exceed this length, a dynamic allocation (\texttt{malloc}) would be needed instead.

In our implementation, we define a constant \texttt{MAX\_PATH} for buffer size[60]. Each time we descend into a subdirectory, we form the next path string, call the recursive function, and then continue the loop. Because we use a local buffer, and each recursive call has its own buffer on the stack, we do not need to free memory for these path strings – they are cleaned up when the function returns. If we had \texttt{malloc}'d them, we would need to ensure \texttt{free} is called after returning from recursion to avoid leaks.

\subsection*{Pseudo-Code Snippet}

To clarify, here is a simplified pseudo-code outline of the recursive search function in C-like syntax:

\begin{lstlisting}
void searchDirectory(const char* basePath, const char* query) {
    DIR* dir = opendir(basePath);
    if (dir == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Cannot open %s\n", basePath);
        return;
    }
    struct dirent* entry;
    while ((entry = readdir(dir)) != NULL) {
        // Skip "." and ".."
        if (strcmp(entry->d_name, ".") == 0 || strcmp(entry->d_name, "..") == 0)
            continue;
        // Build full path
        char newPath[MAX_PATH];
        snprintf(newPath, sizeof(newPath), "%s/%s", basePath, entry->d_name);
        if (entry->d_type == DT_DIR) {
            // Recurse into subdirectory
            searchDirectory(newPath, query);
        } else if (entry->d_type == DT_REG) {
            // Check file name match
            if (strcmp(entry->d_name, query) == 0) {
                printf("Found: %s\n", newPath);
            }
        }
    }
    closedir(dir);
}
\end{lstlisting}

This snippet demonstrates many aspects of C in action: manual error handling (checking for \texttt{NULL} from \texttt{opendir}), use of a pointer to a structure (\texttt{struct dirent*}) for reading entries, string operations for path building, and recursion.

It’s important to note that on different systems, constants like \texttt{DT\_DIR} might not be defined (or \texttt{d\_type} might always be \texttt{DT\_UNKNOWN} on some filesystems), so a robust program might call \texttt{stat()} on \texttt{newPath} to determine if it’s a directory.

Additionally, if one wanted to search file contents (like \texttt{grep}), then upon encountering a regular file, the code would open the file (e.g., with \texttt{fopen}) and scan its contents for the query string. That would involve reading data in, perhaps line by line with \texttt{fgets}, and using \texttt{strstr} or similar to find the substring. Those details extend beyond the current scope, but are implementable with C’s standard I/O library functions.

\subsection*{Compiling and Running}

After writing the C source for this utility (say in \texttt{search.c}), one would compile it using a compiler command, for example:

\begin{verbatim}
gcc -o search search.c
\end{verbatim}

This produces an executable \texttt{search}. Running it might look like:

\begin{verbatim}
./search /home/user/documents report.txt
\end{verbatim}

to search for files named \texttt{"report.txt"} under the documents directory. The program would then print any matches it finds, or nothing if none.

Throughout execution, because C does not have automatic memory management, we made sure to close directory streams (\texttt{closedir}) to release resources. We also avoided dynamic memory except for what \texttt{opendir}/\texttt{readdir} allocate internally.

The recursive nature means deep directory trees could use deep call stacks – in C, large recursion depth can risk stack overflow if uncontrolled, since each recursive call consumes stack memory. For extremely deep searches, an iterative approach or tail recursion (which GCC can optimize in simple cases) might be considered, but for typical filesystem depths this is not an issue.

\subsection*{Error Handling}

In C, robust programs must check the result of almost every system call. In our utility, we check \texttt{opendir} and also in a full implementation would check \texttt{entry->d\_type} (some systems might require using \texttt{lstat} to handle symbolic links properly, etc.). If an error occurs (like a directory can’t be opened due to permissions), we printed to \texttt{stderr} and returned.

We could also set a global error count or propagate an error code up the recursion, but simplicity often suffices: print an error message and skip that branch. C does not throw exceptions, so handling errors tends to be explicit and sometimes verbose, but it ensures the programmer thinks about possible failure modes (file not found, permission denied, etc.).

Through this example, we see how C allows us to directly interface with operating system services (files and directories), using pointers and simple data structures to manage state (the \texttt{DIR*} and \texttt{dirent} structures). The code is efficient – only doing what's necessary – but the burden is on the programmer to manage details correctly (string lengths, closing resources, avoiding infinite recursion). With careful coding and testing, the result is a small, fast utility that exemplifies the power of C in systems programming.

